	"notes": "Webflow's homepage is a stack of full-width bands, the shape most marketing pages use, but with one real twist: the content never gets capped or centred, even on a huge ultrawide monitor — the scan shows it using the full width of the browser at every desktop size tested, unlike Apple's or Linear's homepages, which both stop growing and add quiet margin once the screen gets very wide. The one part that does misbehave on a smaller screen is a row of six small highlight cards: on a laptop the last card gets sliced off at the edge of the window instead of moving to a new row, and it only appears in full once the screen is wide enough. On a phone the whole page becomes one plain column and the menu turns into a hamburger icon. A cookie-consent box also sits in the corner of every screenshot here, which is worth knowing is not part of the page's own layout.",
